<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1834 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hey, Slavs</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="cs">Hej, Slováci</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of 1834 for all Slavic peoples, sung to the tune of Poland's anthem, that became the national anthem of Yugoslavia.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Samuel Tomášik</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (sung to the tune of &quot;Mazurek Dąbrowskiego&quot;, 1797)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Slavs; the Pan-Slavic movement, later Yugoslavia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Czech, Slovak, Serbo-Croatian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Prague, Czech Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>2 November 1834</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Samuel Tomášik, 1834)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a>&quot; is a patriotic song dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and widely regarded as the Pan-Slavic anthem. The Slovak Lutheran minister Samuel Tomášik wrote it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on Sunday, 2 November 1834, at first in Czech and under the title &quot;Hey, Slovaks&quot;. By his diary's account, he was dismayed to hear more German than Czech in the streets of Prague and feared for his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>; the old Polish song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> has not yet perished&quot; came to mind, and he wrote three verses by candlelight in pencil, directing that they be sung to its melody, Dąbrowski's Mazurka of 1797. The composer of the version later used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, slower and with a few changed notes, is not known; modern performances often use a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> arrangement by Oskar Danon.</p><p>Tomášik soon widened the words to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, and the song became a rallying song of Slav nationalism and Pan-Slavic feeling, above all in the West Slavic lands ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Printed in magazines and calendars and sung at political meetings, it became the unofficial anthem of the Pan-Slavic movement and the anthem of the Sokol (&quot;falcon&quot;) gymnastics movement across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. In 1905 a large crowd sang it at the unveiling of the monument to the Slovene poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france-preseren">France Prešeren</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Slavic soldiers on opposite sides of the front sometimes sang it to one another to show common feeling and avoid bloodshed. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> &quot;Hey, Slovaks&quot; has long been an unofficial anthem, above all in times of revolution, though &quot;Nad Tatrou sa blýska&quot; became the official Slovak anthem; the ruling party of the clerofascist Slovak Republic of 1939–45 used it, but that state had no official anthem.</p><p>In the South Slav lands it first appeared in the Illyrian movement, in Dragutin Rakovac's translation &quot;Hey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>&quot;. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in April 1941, the anti-royalist Partisans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> and the Communist Party avoided the royal anthem and sang &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a>&quot; instead, at both sessions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-council-for-the-national-liberation-of-yugoslavia">AVNOJ</a>, their legislative body. It became the de facto anthem of the new Yugoslavia. No other anthem was ever agreed: a law of 1977 named it the temporary anthem, and on 25 November 1988 Amendment IX to the constitution made it the national anthem in law. It served Yugoslavia for 48 years, from 1943 to 1992.</p><p>After the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1991 and 1992 it remained the anthem of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Montenegro, and of the State Union of Serbia and Montenegro from 2003, since the two could not agree on new symbols; many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> disliked it and booed it at football matches and other events. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> had wished to make it Serbia's anthem, but the Socialist Party of Serbia chose &quot;March on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a>&quot;. The state union ended in 2006, and the last official performance was at the 2006 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">FIFA World Cup</a>, where Serbia and Montenegro's team played though the country had dissolved days before. Organisers have since sometimes played it for Serbian teams in error, as in 2015 for the racing driver Dušan Borković. Bijelo Dugme recorded it in 1984 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Laibach</a> in 2006.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A rallying song declaring that the Slavic peoples and their languages live on and will endure against foreign domination; proud and defiant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Czech</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Czech</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Hej, Slováci, ešte naša slovenská reč žije,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hey, Slovaks, our Slovak speech still lives,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Dokiaľ naše verné srdce za náš národ bije.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As long as our faithful heart beats for our nation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Žije, žije, duch slovenský, bude žiť naveky,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It lives, it lives, the Slovak spirit, it will live for ever,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Hrom a peklo, márne vaše proti nám sú vzteky!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thunder and hell, your rages against us are in vain!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Jazyka dar zveril nám Boh, Boh náš hromovládny,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God entrusted to us the gift of language, God our ruler of thunder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Nesmie nám ho teda vyrvať na tom svete žiadny;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So no one in this world may tear it from us;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">I nechže je koľko ľudí, toľko čertov v svete;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And let there be as many devils in the world as there are people;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Boh je s nami: kto proti nám, toho Parom zmetie.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God is with us: whoever is against us, Parom will sweep him away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">A nechže sa i nad nami hrozná búrka vznesie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And even if a terrible storm should rise above us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Skala puká, dub sa láme a zem nech sa trasie;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The rock splits, the oak breaks, and let the earth shake;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">My stojíme stále pevne, ako múry hradné.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We stand ever firm, like the walls of a castle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="cs">Čierna zem pohltí toho, kto odstúpi zradne!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The black earth will swallow him who treacherously deserts!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Samo Tomášik (1834). Text from <a href="https://sk.wikisource.org/wiki/Hej%2C_Slov%C3%A1ci" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 2.2: 'Hrom a peklo' (thunder and hell) is addressed to the song's enemies as an oath-like cry.</li><li>line 3.1: 'hromovládny' = ruling the thunder, thunder-wielding.</li><li>line 4.2: 'Parom' is the Slovak name of Perun, the old Slavic god of thunder; kept as a name.</li><li>line 6.2: 'kto odstúpi zradne' = whoever steps back (falls away) treacherously.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
